    std::cout << n;
    while (n > 1) {
        if (n % 2 == 0) {
            n /= 2;
        } else {
            n = 3 * n + 1;
        }
        ++steps;
        std::cout << ' ' << n;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    std::cout << "Steps: " << steps << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
A `while` loop can take a whole number apart one digit at a time. Two integer operations do the work:

- `n % 10` is the last digit: `4096 % 10` is 6.
- `n / 10` drops the last digit: `4096 / 10` is 409, because int division truncates.

Repeat until `n` reaches 0. To build a reversed number, shift the result one place left and add the digit: `r = r * 10 + digit` gives 6, 69, 690, 6904.

```cpp
const int original = 4096;
int n = original;  // a copy: original keeps 4096
while (n > 0) {
    const int digit = n % 10;
    n /= 10;
}
```

The loop destroys `n`, so save the original first when you need it later. `int n = original;` copies the value into a separate `int` with its own 4 bytes, so shrinking `n` never touches `original`. Reset the copy before a second loop.

Gotchas: a digit product starts at 1, not 0. Reversing drops trailing zeros (1200 becomes 21) and can overflow `int` for large numbers. For a negative `n`, `n % 10` is negative and `n > 0` is false, so these loops handle zero and positive numbers only.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const int original = 4096;

    int n = original;
    int digitSum = 0;
    while (n > 0) {
        digitSum += n % 10;
        n /= 10;
    }
    std::cout << "Digit sum: " << digitSum << '\n';

    n = original;
    int reversed = 0;
    while (n > 0) {
        const int digit = n % 10;
        reversed = reversed * 10 + digit;
        n /= 10;
    }
    std::cout << "Reversed: " << reversed << '\n';

    if (reversed == original) {
        std::cout << original << " is a palindrome\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << original << " is not a palindrome\n";
    }
    return 0;
}

