steps.body:
`do { } while (condition);` runs the body first and checks the condition after, so the body always runs at least once. The semicolon after the closing parenthesis is required.

That fixes counting digits. With a plain `while (n > 0)`, the number 0 never enters the loop and reports 0 digits. A do-while counts one digit before it checks:

```cpp
int n = 0;
int digits = 0;
do {
    ++digits;
    n /= 10;
} while (n > 0);  // digits is 1
```

When the stop test belongs in the middle of the body, write `while (true)` and leave with `break`. It reads well for searches: keep trying the next value until one fits.

```cpp
int x = 1;
while (true) {
    if (x * x > 50) {
        break;  // x is 8
    }
    ++x;
}
```

Every `while (true)` needs a `break` that is sure to run, or it is an infinite loop. `break` only leaves the innermost loop, just as in a `for`.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const int original = 0;
    int n = original;
    int digits = 0;
    do {
        ++digits;
        n /= 10;
    } while (n > 0);
    std::cout << "Digits in " << original << ": " << digits << '\n';

    int power = 1;
    while (true) {
        power *= 2;
        if (power > 1000) {
            break;
        }
    }
    std::cout << "First power of 2 above 1000: " << power << '\n';
    return 0;
}

